.TH PWD 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
pwd \- print the current directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pwd
.RB [ \-LP ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pwd
prints the name of the current directory, followed by a newline, on
standard output.
It reads no input and ignores operands.
.PP
The name is absolute, with a drive letter and forward slashes, as in
.IR C:/Users/me/src .
The root of a drive is
.IR C:/ .
A network share entered without a drive letter gives a UNC name, as in
.IR //host/share/dir .
.PP
It is the name that Windows holds for the process's current directory,
as set by the program that changed to it or started the process.
.B pwd
neither resolves links in it nor changes its case.
When the kit's shell changes directory, it uppercases the drive letter
or UNC host name, matches the capitalization stored on disk, and
resolves a symbolic link or junction that is the last component of the
new directory, so the name can differ from the shell's
.BR PWD .
.B cmd.exe
keeps the drive letter as typed, which may be lowercase, and keeps a
junction in the name.
.PP
The kit's
.BR sh (1)
has a
.B pwd
built-in of its own, which runs in place of this program; see
.BR NOTES .
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-LP ,
repeated, and given after operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
Any other option is an error.
.TP
.B \-L
Print the value of
.B PWD
instead of the name from Windows, when
.B PWD
names the current directory.
.B PWD
must pass all of these tests:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
It begins with a forward slash.
A name with a drive letter never qualifies.
.IP \(bu 2
It has no
.B .\&
or
.B ..\&
component, where components are separated by forward slashes.
.IP \(bu 2
It names the current directory, which is judged by whether Windows
reports the same volume and file index for both.
.RE
.IP
When any of these fails, or
.B PWD
is unset,
.B \-L
has no effect, and there is no message.
Otherwise
.B PWD
is printed exactly as it is, with no cleanup.
.IP
The kit's shell sets
.B PWD
with a drive letter, except on a network share, so
.B \-L
usually prints the same name as without it.
It differs when
.B PWD
begins with a slash, as for a name relative to the root of the drive,
such as
.IR /Users/me ,
or a UNC name, such as
.IR //host/share/dir .
.TP
.B \-P
Print the name from Windows.
This is the default.
Giving
.B \-P
does not cancel
.BR \-L :
with both, in either order,
.B \-L
applies.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are then ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PWD
Read only with
.BR \-L ,
as described there.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown option, or a failure to get the name of the current
directory.
.TP
.B 255
The output could not be written, as when standard output is closed or is
a pipe whose reader has exited.
No message is printed.
.PP
An unknown option is reported on standard error, followed by a usage
message.
A failure to get the name is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
pwd: getcwd: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the current directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cd C:/Windows
$ pwd
C:/Windows
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print it with backslashes, for a program that wants them.
Naming
.B busybox
runs this program instead of the shell's built-in:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ busybox pwd | tr / \(aq\e\e\(aq
C:\eWindows
.EE
.RE
.PP
Set
.B PWD
to a name relative to the root of the drive.
With
.BR \-L ,
it is printed when it names the current directory, and otherwise
ignored:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cd C:/Windows
$ PWD=/Windows busybox pwd \-L
/Windows
$ PWD=/Users busybox pwd \-L
C:/Windows
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
The kit's
.BR sh (1)
runs its own
.B pwd
built-in, not this program, in scripts and at the prompt.
The built-in has its own options, including
.BR \-a ,
which this program lacks; see
.BR sh (1).
This program is what the
.I pwd.exe
launcher in the kit's
.I bin
runs, as when
.BR cmd.exe ,
.BR env ,
or another program starts
.BR pwd ,
and what
.B busybox pwd
runs; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
Differences from POSIX and GNU
.BR pwd :
.IP \(bu 2
The default is
.BR \-P ,
not
.BR \-L ,
and
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
does not change it.
.IP \(bu 2
Unlike GNU
.BR "pwd \-P" ,
.B \-P
does not resolve symbolic links; see
.BR DESCRIPTION .
.IP \(bu 2
Operands are ignored without a warning.
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options other than
.BR \-\-help ,
so
.B \-\-logical
and
.B \-\-version
are errors, and
.B \-\-help
is recognized only as the first argument.
.PP
On Windows 10 version 1903 and later, the name may contain any Unicode
characters.
On older versions it is limited to the system code page.
.PP
The output line ends with LF, not CRLF.
When standard output is a console, the result is converted for display
from the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR realpath (1),
.BR sh (1)
